// MAY THIS RECEIPT'S PEAK BE READ AS A DEMAND, OR ONLY AS A LOWER BOUND? This is the question
// the swap policy exists to answer, and the reason the policy is carried on the receipt rather
// than described beside it. The rule the corpus already applies to a CHARGE at its throttle line
// -- a pinned reading says "at least this much" and nothing about how far past it the workload
// wanted to go -- applies to a HELD SET on the swap axis for the same reason, and neither the
// 2026-09-19 nor the 2026-09-21 receipt escapes it.
//
// THE ORDER IS SEVERITY, NOT CONVENIENCE, and the first matching cause is the one reported: a run
// that did not finish never reached whatever it would have held later; a peak beat already
// stalling was being held by reclaim when it was sampled; and swap merely AVAILABLE is enough to
// censor, because anonymous pages the workload still wants can leave residency without the
// workload failing. Only a completed run, whose peak beat was not stalling, under a policy that
// cannot spill, reads a demand.
type PeakLowerBoundCause
  = RunDidNotComplete
  | PeakBeatWasStalling { beat: Int, stall: BeatStall }
  | PeakBeatStallUnread { beat: Int }
  | SwapCouldAbsorbAnonymousPages { declared_swap_max: ByteSize }
  | SwapPolicyIsRead { swap_max: ByteSize }
  | SwapHeldPagesAtPeak { beat: Int, swapped: ByteSize }

// THE SUCCESSFUL ARM IS NAMED FOR WHAT IT ESTABLISHES, WHICH IS NOT EXACT DEMAND. It used to be
// called PeakIsDemand, and that name asserted past its evidence in the way DESIGN 4d forbids: the
// arm is reached when the workload completed, the SAMPLED peak showed no stall, and swap either
// could not spill or, read on the leaf at the peak beat, held none of its pages. Those remove two CENSORING mechanisms -- they do not make a sampled maximum the true
// maximum. memory.stat carries no high-water counter, so this receipt's peak is the largest value
// a PERIODIC sample caught, and a spike that rose and fell between two beats is invisible to it.
// This module's own beat-cadence note has said exactly that from the start; the fold promoted
// past it anyway, and the ARM NAME was the assertion, because a later reader consumes an arm
// called PeakIsDemand as a fact rather than as the bet it is.
//
// SO EVERY ARM OF THIS TYPE IS A LOWER BOUND, and they differ only in WHY. The uncensored arm is
// the strongest reading available from this instrument and it is still a floor on demand. An
// exact-demand standing would need a persistent high-water observation of the same held-set
// quantity, or some other construction that cannot miss an intermediate maximum; neither exists
// here, so neither is claimed.
type PeakReadingStanding
  = PeakIsUncensoredSampledMaximum
  | PeakIsLowerBound { cause: PeakLowerBoundCause }
  | PeakUnreadable { cause: PeakUnattributableCause }

// MATCHES receipt_held_set_peak DIRECTLY, not receipt_peak_beat, and the difference is a false
// cause. receipt_peak_beat collapses HeldSetPeakUnrepresentable into Absent, so routing through it
// reported a TORN receipt as ReceiptHasNoBeats -- a located diagnostic naming a fact that is not
// true of a receipt which plainly has beats, and it left ReceiptTorn unreachable from this fold
// entirely (review 69767). That is this module's own stated bar turned against it: four causes,
// four different remedies, and a cause naming the wrong thing is worse than an unlocated one.
fn receipt_peak_reading_standing(r: FloorHeldSetReceipt) -> PeakReadingStanding {
  match receipt_held_set_peak(r: r) {
    HeldSetPeakNoBeats => PeakUnreadable { cause: ReceiptHasNoBeats }
    HeldSetPeakUnrepresentable { cause: c } => PeakUnreadable { cause: ReceiptTorn { overflow: c } }
    HeldSetPeakAt { beat: b, bytes: _ } =>
      if !r.run_completed {
        PeakIsLowerBound { cause: RunDidNotComplete }
      } else {
        if beat_stall_is_positive(b: b) {
          PeakIsLowerBound { cause: PeakBeatWasStalling { beat: b.beat, stall: b.stall } }
        } else if !beat_stall_is_zero(b: b) {
          PeakIsLowerBound { cause: PeakBeatStallUnread { beat: b.beat } }
        } else {
          match b.swap {
            SwapRead { bytes: sw } =>
              if byte_size_count(b: sw) == 0 {
                PeakIsUncensoredSampledMaximum
              } else {
                PeakIsLowerBound { cause: SwapHeldPagesAtPeak { beat: b.beat, swapped: sw } }
              }
            SwapUnread => peak_standing_from_swap_policy(policy: r.swap_policy)
          }
        }
      }
  }
}

// WITH NO LEAF SWAP READING, THE POLICY IS ALL THERE IS TO JUDGE BY: a slot that cannot swap
// cannot have spilled, and a slot that can -- declared or read -- might have, so the peak stands
// as a lower bound naming which. This was the whole rule until beats carried the leaf's own swap.
fn peak_standing_from_swap_policy(policy: RunSwapPolicy) -> PeakReadingStanding {
  match policy {
    SwapDisabled => PeakIsUncensoredSampledMaximum
    SwapMaxRead { swap_max: m } =>
      if byte_size_count(b: m) == 0 { PeakIsUncensoredSampledMaximum } else { PeakIsLowerBound { cause: SwapPolicyIsRead { swap_max: m } } }
    SwapMaxDeclaredUnread { declared: d, declaring_authority: _ } =>
      PeakIsLowerBound { cause: SwapCouldAbsorbAnonymousPages { declared_swap_max: d } }
  }
}

// THE 2026-09-23 RECEIPT, SUCCESSOR TO gunbc_floor_memory_stat_receipt_2026_09_21, FROM THE FIRST RUN
// WHOSE BEATS CARRY A READING AT EVERY PHASE BOUNDARY AND THE LEAF'S OWN SWAP (required-floor run
// 35815214135, job 107035188664, runner unit actions-runner@srv4-14.service, a pull_request run of
// gunbc#12088 at 7c97cb2b62). WHAT IT MEASURES IS A COMBINED BASE, and the base is named so a reader
// cannot credit either change with the other's share: main after gunbc#12081 (the floor reads the
// corpus once for both prepares) plus gunbc#12088 (the diff observer resolves gunbc.ci_diff_policy
// instead of the whole CI spec), with gunbc#12078's instrument.
//
// ROWS ARE EVERY stat beat the run printed, in the order printed, and `beat` is the SAMPLE'S ORDINAL in
// that order -- not the watchdog's minute count, because seam beats sit between watchdog beats and
// later_unstalled_before orders by this field. The instrument's `when=` token for each row is the
// comment above it. Seam beats close no stall window and so carry StallUnread; every row read its
// leaf memory.swap.current.
//
// WHAT IT ESTABLISHES, BY ITS OWN FOLDS rather than by a sentence here: receipt_peak_reading_standing
// answers PeakIsUncensoredSampledMaximum -- the workload ran to its end, the peak beat read a zero
// stall, and its leaf swap read zero, so neither censoring mechanism the 2026-09-19 and 2026-09-21
// readings carried was acting at the peak. That is still a SAMPLED maximum and a lower bound on true
// demand (PeakReadingStanding's own note), at 60 s cadence plus the seam beats.
//
// WHAT IT DOES NOT ESTABLISH. It is ONE WARM REPEATED run on a CI host slot -- the job's toolchain
// build hit the shared compilation cache -- so it says nothing about a cold attempt, whose accounting
// is gunbc.floor_cold_build_receipt's and is not mixed in here. It ran at the host slot's 26 GiB row,
// not at the 28 GiB shakedown slot, and not in a fresh attempt cgroup: the leaf is the runner
